\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.8}{\ignorespaces Test cross-entropy of the base NNUE against training-set size. From this plot we can infer that a NNUE model with two hidden layers of 64 neurons does not overfit a dataset larger than 3 million positions, though accuracy can still marginally increase with a bigger dataset.}}{65}{figure.caption.32}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.9}{\ignorespaces Test cross-entropy of the base NNUE against training-set size. A NNUE model with two hidden layers of 256 neurons does not overfit a dataset larger than 7 million positions.}}{65}{figure.caption.33}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.10}{\ignorespaces Cross-entropy trajectory during the expert fine-tuning phase using the Rprop optimizer. This figure shows that the majority of the buckets see relatively little improvement, while the a few buckets are responsible for most of the gains. The analysis shows that some \textit {lucky} experts get to train on a mix of simpler positions.}}{67}{figure.caption.34}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.11}{\ignorespaces Cross-entropy trajectory during the expert fine-tuning phase using the Rprop optimizer. The plot illustrates the stable convergence and the characteristic adaptive step-size mechanism of Rprop, which mitigates instability by stepping back when the loss temporarily increases, allowing the specialized heads to properly learn without collapsing.}}{68}{figure.caption.35}%
